edb/ref 17592186522645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522795 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522795 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522795 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not get enough sleep, or you will be alert the next day.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e24afd3b-3cd1-54ee-83ee-26ca9c096040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522795 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not get enough sleep.\n\n- $B:$ You will be alert the next day.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You get enough sleep.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you get enough sleep, then you will be alert the next day.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you get enough sleep, you will be alert the next day.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522818 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522818 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0450a032-0f52-5ba5-abe8-82e9866f5d76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522818 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522846 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "623df059-54b2-5e71-bdb3-85aed237cbf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522846 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522879 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522